1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,399
It's six o'clock, and it's still ninety

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:03,520
five degrees.

3
00:00:04,281 --> 00:00:06,601
Lena blows the whistle, and everybody has

4
00:00:06,641 --> 00:00:07,801
to get out of the pool.

5
00:00:08,532 --> 00:00:10,977
She's the lifeguard, and she thinks closing

6
00:00:11,057 --> 00:00:12,577
this early is a mistake.

7
00:00:13,343 --> 00:00:15,503
So she asks the city council to keep the

8
00:00:15,583 --> 00:00:17,263
pool open until nine.

9
00:00:18,044 --> 00:00:20,524
One council member leans forward and asks:

10
00:00:20,955 --> 00:00:24,390
Do the town's teenagers actually want that?

11
00:00:24,470 --> 00:00:25,670
You'll need to bring us data.

12
00:00:27,170 --> 00:00:29,570
By the end, you'll turn Lena's survey into

13
00:00:29,730 --> 00:00:32,570
one honest sentence the council can trust.

14
00:00:33,306 --> 00:00:35,386
On the way, you'll see what statistics is

15
00:00:35,546 --> 00:00:37,626
for, how to read a data table,

16
00:00:37,746 --> 00:00:39,866
and how to get a percent right by picking

17
00:00:39,906 --> 00:00:40,746
the right whole.

18
00:00:41,946 --> 00:00:43,786
So why do we start here?

19
00:00:44,586 --> 00:00:46,986
Five words come back in every unit of this

20
00:00:47,066 --> 00:00:50,141
course: population, sample,

21
00:00:50,221 --> 00:00:54,266
variable, parameter, and statistic.

22
00:00:54,306 --> 00:00:57,047
And on the exam, a correct number with no

23
00:00:57,127 --> 00:01:00,312
context can still lose the point.

24
00:01:00,392 --> 00:01:03,192
Get these right now, and the rest gets easier.

25
00:01:06,992 --> 00:01:09,312
Some questions have one answer you can just

26
00:01:09,392 --> 00:01:10,192
look up.

27
00:01:10,733 --> 00:01:12,293
What time does the pool close?

28
00:01:13,013 --> 00:01:15,633
It closes at six, and that's the end of

29
00:01:15,673 --> 00:01:16,473
it.

30
00:01:16,595 --> 00:01:18,675
Others have answers that change from person

31
00:01:18,755 --> 00:01:19,555
to person.

32
00:01:20,256 --> 00:01:22,256
How many hours a week do teenagers in this

33
00:01:22,336 --> 00:01:23,456
town swim?

34
00:01:24,151 --> 00:01:25,831
Nobody can just look that up.

35
00:01:26,588 --> 00:01:29,508
That second kind is what statistics is for.

36
00:01:30,673 --> 00:01:33,153
So statistics is how we collect data,

37
00:01:33,233 --> 00:01:35,919
sum it up, and make sense of it...

38
00:01:35,999 --> 00:01:37,279
when the answers vary.

39
00:01:38,079 --> 00:01:41,039
Data are the values you record: answers,

40
00:01:41,319 --> 00:01:42,639
counts, measurements.

41
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:46,550
So a quick test for a good question is this:

42
00:01:46,630 --> 00:01:48,630
would the answer differ from one person

43
00:01:48,710 --> 00:01:49,630
to the next?

44
00:01:50,721 --> 00:01:53,606
You'll hear two words all year.

45
00:01:53,686 --> 00:01:56,166
The population is the whole group a question

46
00:01:56,246 --> 00:01:57,776
is about.

47
00:01:57,896 --> 00:01:59,936
The sample is the part of that group you

48
00:02:00,016 --> 00:02:02,576
actually collect data from.

49
00:02:02,656 --> 00:02:04,627
Data come from the sample,

50
00:02:04,667 --> 00:02:07,347
and the conclusion goes back out to everyone.

51
00:02:07,547 --> 00:02:10,367
So why don't we just ask everyone?

52
00:02:10,447 --> 00:02:13,697
Usually it costs too much or takes too long,

53
00:02:13,817 --> 00:02:16,918
because a country has millions of people.

54
00:02:16,958 --> 00:02:19,238
But a sample only helps if it looks like

55
00:02:19,278 --> 00:02:22,323
the population in the ways that matter.

56
00:02:22,403 --> 00:02:24,403
What if you ask only the kids already at

57
00:02:24,443 --> 00:02:25,963
the pool?

58
00:02:26,083 --> 00:02:28,403
Of course they'll love longer hours,

59
00:02:28,483 --> 00:02:31,323
so that sample leans one way from the start.

60
00:02:32,804 --> 00:02:35,629
So that night, Lena makes a plan.

61
00:02:36,315 --> 00:02:38,555
Her question is about every teenager in

62
00:02:38,635 --> 00:02:40,050
town.

63
00:02:40,130 --> 00:02:42,476
So how does she reach them?

64
00:02:42,556 --> 00:02:45,431
Nearly all of them go to the one high school,

65
00:02:45,511 --> 00:02:47,271
so she narrows her question to the high

66
00:02:47,351 --> 00:02:49,921
school's students, and uses the school's

67
00:02:50,001 --> 00:02:52,241
list of all two thousand of them.

68
00:02:52,927 --> 00:02:55,007
She has a computer pick one hundred fifty

69
00:02:55,087 --> 00:02:56,207
names at random.

70
00:02:56,958 --> 00:02:59,518
Nobody chooses who's in, so no group gets

71
00:02:59,598 --> 00:03:00,793
favored...

72
00:03:00,833 --> 00:03:02,673
and the sample is likely to look like the

73
00:03:02,713 --> 00:03:03,513
school.

74
00:03:04,049 --> 00:03:07,139
Her population is the two thousand students,

75
00:03:07,179 --> 00:03:09,059
and her sample is the one hundred fifty

76
00:03:09,139 --> 00:03:10,829
she'll ask.

77
00:03:10,909 --> 00:03:13,469
From now on, her answer speaks for the school's

78
00:03:13,709 --> 00:03:17,370
students, not every teen in town.

79
00:03:17,490 --> 00:03:19,610
Over the next week, she asks every one of

80
00:03:19,690 --> 00:03:21,850
them the same question.

81
00:03:21,930 --> 00:03:23,810
Would you swim between six and nine,

82
00:03:23,850 --> 00:03:26,741
if the pool stayed open?

83
00:03:26,821 --> 00:03:29,181
She counts the answers, and ninety six out

84
00:03:29,221 --> 00:03:32,201
of one hundred fifty say yes.

85
00:03:32,281 --> 00:03:34,361
When she divides ninety six by one hundred

86
00:03:34,441 --> 00:03:35,961
fifty, she gets...

87
00:03:36,041 --> 00:03:38,436
sixty four percent.

88
00:03:38,516 --> 00:03:40,836
So can she tell the council that exactly

89
00:03:40,996 --> 00:03:42,916
sixty four percent of the school's students

90
00:03:43,036 --> 00:03:45,856
want evening hours?

91
00:03:45,936 --> 00:03:47,736
No, she can't say that.

92
00:03:48,477 --> 00:03:50,397
If she picked a second random group of one

93
00:03:50,477 --> 00:03:53,217
hundred fifty, would exactly ninety six

94
00:03:53,297 --> 00:03:55,287
say yes again?

95
00:03:55,367 --> 00:03:58,007
Maybe ninety one would, or maybe a hundred

96
00:03:58,087 --> 00:03:58,887
would.

97
00:03:59,293 --> 00:04:01,533
Random samples from the same population

98
00:04:01,613 --> 00:04:03,803
tend to land close to each other,

99
00:04:03,883 --> 00:04:05,483
but they're rarely identical.

100
00:04:06,199 --> 00:04:09,124
That's called sampling variability.

101
00:04:09,204 --> 00:04:12,004
So sixty four percent is a fact about the

102
00:04:12,164 --> 00:04:12,964
sample.

103
00:04:13,585 --> 00:04:15,825
For the whole school, it's an estimate,

104
00:04:15,905 --> 00:04:17,305
give or take a few points.

105
00:04:18,456 --> 00:04:20,976
And you always say it in context.

106
00:04:21,776 --> 00:04:23,856
So what is context?

107
00:04:24,641 --> 00:04:27,521
It's the real situation behind the number:

108
00:04:27,601 --> 00:04:29,681
who was studied, and what was measured.

109
00:04:30,467 --> 00:04:32,947
If you just say sixty four percent,

110
00:04:33,027 --> 00:04:35,262
what does the reader learn?

111
00:04:35,342 --> 00:04:37,252
They learn nothing.

112
00:04:37,332 --> 00:04:39,492
But if you say that sixty four percent of

113
00:04:39,572 --> 00:04:41,652
the one hundred fifty students surveyed

114
00:04:41,772 --> 00:04:43,492
said they'd swim in the evening,

115
00:04:43,572 --> 00:04:46,532
now they know who, what, and how many.

116
00:04:47,288 --> 00:04:50,168
On the exam, a bare number usually loses

117
00:04:50,248 --> 00:04:52,368
credit, even when it's right.

118
00:04:53,568 --> 00:04:54,768
Now it's your turn.

119
00:04:55,568 --> 00:04:57,728
A cafeteria manager wants to know whether

120
00:04:57,808 --> 00:04:59,728
the school's eight hundred students would

121
00:04:59,848 --> 00:05:01,488
buy a salad bar lunch.

122
00:05:02,288 --> 00:05:04,288
She picks fifty students at random,

123
00:05:04,368 --> 00:05:05,808
and twenty say yes.

124
00:05:06,598 --> 00:05:09,478
Name the population and the sample.

125
00:05:09,678 --> 00:05:12,158
Then write the result in context.

126
00:05:19,361 --> 00:05:22,661
The population is all eight hundred students,

127
00:05:22,821 --> 00:05:24,581
because the question is about every one

128
00:05:24,661 --> 00:05:25,621
of them.

129
00:05:25,781 --> 00:05:27,181
So who's the sample?

130
00:05:27,937 --> 00:05:29,777
It's the fifty who were asked,

131
00:05:29,897 --> 00:05:33,377
and twenty out of fifty is forty percent.

132
00:05:33,457 --> 00:05:35,857
In context, you'd write that forty percent

133
00:05:35,897 --> 00:05:38,097
of the fifty students surveyed said they'd

134
00:05:38,177 --> 00:05:39,937
buy a salad bar lunch.

135
00:05:40,017 --> 00:05:41,937
That suggests about forty percent of the

136
00:05:42,097 --> 00:05:43,457
school would, give or take.

137
00:05:44,257 --> 00:05:47,404
The trap is calling the fifty the population,

138
00:05:47,484 --> 00:05:49,404
just because that's who you talked to.

139
00:05:53,204 --> 00:05:55,364
Data usually arrive as a table.

140
00:05:56,164 --> 00:05:58,884
Each row is one thing the data are about,

141
00:05:59,299 --> 00:06:01,939
and we call that an observational unit.

142
00:06:02,715 --> 00:06:04,555
So what can a unit be?

143
00:06:05,241 --> 00:06:07,997
It can be a person, a dog,

144
00:06:08,077 --> 00:06:09,337
a car...

145
00:06:09,457 --> 00:06:10,257
even a day.

146
00:06:10,937 --> 00:06:14,917
So in a data table, each row is one observational

147
00:06:15,077 --> 00:06:15,877
unit.

148
00:06:16,762 --> 00:06:19,322
A variable is a characteristic you record

149
00:06:19,402 --> 00:06:22,638
for every unit, one whose value can change

150
00:06:22,718 --> 00:06:23,958
from unit to unit.

151
00:06:24,669 --> 00:06:28,874
In a table, each column is one variable.

152
00:06:28,954 --> 00:06:31,880
Eye color is a variable, and brown is one

153
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:34,610
of its values.

154
00:06:34,690 --> 00:06:37,015
Here's Lena's spreadsheet.

155
00:06:37,095 --> 00:06:39,415
Each row is one student she asked,

156
00:06:39,495 --> 00:06:42,515
so a student is the observational unit,

157
00:06:42,635 --> 00:06:45,661
and there are one hundred fifty rows.

158
00:06:45,781 --> 00:06:48,576
And what did she record for each one?

159
00:06:48,656 --> 00:06:51,456
Each row has six columns: grade,

160
00:06:51,576 --> 00:06:53,751
side of town, whether they'd swim in the

161
00:06:53,831 --> 00:06:56,956
evening, hours swum last week,

162
00:06:57,076 --> 00:06:58,716
whether they hold a season pass,

163
00:06:58,796 --> 00:07:00,116
and home zip code.

164
00:07:00,812 --> 00:07:03,512
That's six variables, and they're not all

165
00:07:03,552 --> 00:07:04,672
the same kind.

166
00:07:06,103 --> 00:07:08,503
A categorical variable puts each unit in

167
00:07:08,583 --> 00:07:09,383
a group.

168
00:07:09,863 --> 00:07:12,063
Side of town is north or south.

169
00:07:12,779 --> 00:07:15,229
Evening swim is yes or no,

170
00:07:15,309 --> 00:07:16,669
and so is season pass.

171
00:07:17,469 --> 00:07:19,709
A quantitative variable records a number

172
00:07:19,789 --> 00:07:22,979
that's an amount, so hours swum last week

173
00:07:23,059 --> 00:07:24,219
is quantitative.

174
00:07:24,930 --> 00:07:27,170
The test is to ask whether an average would

175
00:07:27,250 --> 00:07:28,090
make sense.

176
00:07:28,756 --> 00:07:31,076
Would average hours swum mean something?

177
00:07:31,876 --> 00:07:34,496
Sure it would, so that's quantitative.

178
00:07:35,296 --> 00:07:37,371
Grade, nine through twelve,

179
00:07:37,411 --> 00:07:38,451
is a number too.

180
00:07:39,137 --> 00:07:41,137
But here it names a class year,

181
00:07:41,217 --> 00:07:43,517
so Lena uses it as a group,

182
00:07:43,597 --> 00:07:45,197
which makes it categorical.

183
00:07:46,358 --> 00:07:48,558
Now comes the classic trap.

184
00:07:49,358 --> 00:07:52,339
zip codes are written as numbers.

185
00:07:52,459 --> 00:07:55,944
But what's the average of two zip codes?

186
00:07:56,024 --> 00:07:58,369
It's a meaningless number.

187
00:07:58,449 --> 00:08:02,799
A zip code is a label, so it's categorical.

188
00:08:02,879 --> 00:08:05,519
Jersey numbers, phone area codes and locker

189
00:08:05,719 --> 00:08:07,920
numbers work the same way.

190
00:08:07,960 --> 00:08:09,880
They're written as numbers...

191
00:08:09,960 --> 00:08:12,040
but they're used as names.

192
00:08:13,186 --> 00:08:15,986
Quantitative variables come in two kinds.

193
00:08:16,146 --> 00:08:19,066
Some are counts, like siblings or texts

194
00:08:19,146 --> 00:08:20,026
sent today.

195
00:08:20,747 --> 00:08:22,797
You can have two or three,

196
00:08:22,877 --> 00:08:24,517
but never two and a half.

197
00:08:25,238 --> 00:08:26,638
Those are discrete.

198
00:08:27,349 --> 00:08:29,949
Others are measurements, like height or

199
00:08:29,989 --> 00:08:31,349
the time to swim a lap.

200
00:08:32,030 --> 00:08:34,565
They can land anywhere in a range,

201
00:08:34,645 --> 00:08:36,565
and those are continuous.

202
00:08:37,666 --> 00:08:38,906
It's your turn again!

203
00:08:39,627 --> 00:08:41,867
An animal shelter keeps a table with one

204
00:08:41,947 --> 00:08:43,107
row per dog.

205
00:08:43,818 --> 00:08:46,418
The columns are breed, weight in pounds,

206
00:08:46,833 --> 00:08:49,613
number of times adopted, and kennel number.

207
00:08:50,413 --> 00:08:53,124
First, what's the observational unit?

208
00:08:53,915 --> 00:08:56,475
Then, for each column, is it categorical

209
00:08:56,635 --> 00:08:57,595
or quantitative?

210
00:08:58,361 --> 00:09:00,256
And if it's quantitative,

211
00:09:00,336 --> 00:09:02,496
is it discrete or continuous?

212
00:09:10,702 --> 00:09:13,662
There's one row per dog, so a dog is the

213
00:09:13,822 --> 00:09:14,942
observational unit.

214
00:09:15,742 --> 00:09:17,182
Breed is categorical.

215
00:09:17,982 --> 00:09:20,302
Weight is quantitative and continuous,

216
00:09:20,382 --> 00:09:21,422
because it's a measurement.

217
00:09:22,203 --> 00:09:24,443
Number of times adopted is quantitative

218
00:09:24,523 --> 00:09:26,868
and discrete, because it's a count.

219
00:09:27,569 --> 00:09:29,969
And is kennel number quantitative?

220
00:09:30,769 --> 00:09:33,809
No, it's categorical, because it's a label

221
00:09:33,889 --> 00:09:35,809
written as a number, like a zip code.

222
00:09:36,609 --> 00:09:38,929
The average kennel number means nothing.

223
00:09:42,729 --> 00:09:44,809
Now we need the next pair of words.

224
00:09:45,480 --> 00:09:47,720
A number that describes a population is

225
00:09:47,800 --> 00:09:48,600
a parameter.

226
00:09:49,371 --> 00:09:52,251
A number that describes a sample is a statistic.

227
00:09:53,037 --> 00:09:54,477
There's an easy way to remember it.

228
00:09:55,233 --> 00:09:57,838
Population goes with Parameter,

229
00:09:57,918 --> 00:10:00,709
and Sample goes with Statistic,

230
00:10:00,789 --> 00:10:03,329
so P goes with P...

231
00:10:03,409 --> 00:10:04,849
and S goes with S.

232
00:10:06,049 --> 00:10:08,049
Parameters are usually unknown.

233
00:10:08,835 --> 00:10:09,715
And why is that?

234
00:10:10,436 --> 00:10:12,596
To get one, you'd have to measure everyone

235
00:10:12,636 --> 00:10:13,796
in the population.

236
00:10:14,537 --> 00:10:17,477
So you compute a statistic from a sample,

237
00:10:17,557 --> 00:10:19,557
and use it to estimate the parameter.

238
00:10:20,357 --> 00:10:21,637
So which one moves?

239
00:10:22,348 --> 00:10:25,499
A parameter stays fixed, but a statistic

240
00:10:25,659 --> 00:10:27,419
changes from sample to sample.

241
00:10:28,905 --> 00:10:31,305
Lena finds one number she doesn't need to

242
00:10:31,385 --> 00:10:32,911
estimate.

243
00:10:32,951 --> 00:10:35,656
The school sells the season pool passes,

244
00:10:35,736 --> 00:10:37,576
and its records show that thirty percent

245
00:10:37,616 --> 00:10:39,176
of its students hold one.

246
00:10:39,886 --> 00:10:41,526
That's about three in ten.

247
00:10:42,272 --> 00:10:44,992
So is that a parameter or a statistic?

248
00:10:45,668 --> 00:10:47,988
It comes from records on all two thousand

249
00:10:48,068 --> 00:10:50,893
students, the whole population...

250
00:10:50,973 --> 00:10:52,413
so it's a parameter.

251
00:10:53,109 --> 00:10:55,189
In her sample, forty of the one hundred

252
00:10:55,349 --> 00:10:56,709
fifty hold a pass.

253
00:10:57,365 --> 00:10:59,795
That's about twenty seven percent,

254
00:10:59,875 --> 00:11:00,875
a statistic.

255
00:11:01,576 --> 00:11:02,976
Are the two numbers equal?

256
00:11:03,667 --> 00:11:06,297
They're close, but they're not equal,

257
00:11:06,697 --> 00:11:09,028
and that's normal, because every sample

258
00:11:09,108 --> 00:11:10,548
comes out a little different.

259
00:11:11,969 --> 00:11:15,085
There's one more word, and it's distribution.

260
00:11:15,846 --> 00:11:18,086
The distribution of a variable tells you

261
00:11:18,166 --> 00:11:20,961
what values it takes, and how often it takes

262
00:11:21,041 --> 00:11:21,841
each one.

263
00:11:22,437 --> 00:11:25,712
For side of town, it's simple: sixty students

264
00:11:25,792 --> 00:11:29,003
from the north side, ninety from the south,

265
00:11:29,083 --> 00:11:30,443
and a bar chart shows it.

266
00:11:31,138 --> 00:11:33,934
For hours swum, it's how those one hundred

267
00:11:34,094 --> 00:11:36,044
fifty numbers spread out,

268
00:11:36,124 --> 00:11:39,164
from zero hours up to the most anyone swam.

269
00:11:39,870 --> 00:11:42,430
Describing distributions is the job of most

270
00:11:42,470 --> 00:11:44,030
of the rest of Unit 1.

271
00:11:45,176 --> 00:11:46,056
Now you try one.

272
00:11:46,797 --> 00:11:48,637
A school district's records show that the

273
00:11:48,797 --> 00:11:51,117
average commute for all twelve hundred staff

274
00:11:51,197 --> 00:11:53,157
members is twenty two minutes.

275
00:11:53,803 --> 00:11:56,043
A reporter who hasn't seen those records

276
00:11:56,203 --> 00:11:58,718
picks forty staff members at random,

277
00:11:58,798 --> 00:12:01,118
and their average is twenty five minutes.

278
00:12:01,809 --> 00:12:03,869
Which number is the parameter,

279
00:12:03,949 --> 00:12:05,469
and which is the statistic?

280
00:12:06,145 --> 00:12:09,245
And if another reporter took another forty,

281
00:12:09,325 --> 00:12:10,525
which number would change?

282
00:12:17,802 --> 00:12:20,042
Twenty two minutes comes from all twelve

283
00:12:20,082 --> 00:12:22,578
hundred staff, the population.

284
00:12:23,308 --> 00:12:24,828
So it's the parameter.

285
00:12:25,569 --> 00:12:27,729
Twenty five minutes describes the forty

286
00:12:27,809 --> 00:12:30,509
in the sample, so it's a statistic.

287
00:12:31,255 --> 00:12:33,495
A second reporter would likely get a different

288
00:12:33,575 --> 00:12:34,375
average.

289
00:12:34,911 --> 00:12:37,696
The statistic changes, and the twenty two

290
00:12:37,776 --> 00:12:38,576
stays put.

291
00:12:39,202 --> 00:12:41,442
The trap is guessing from the size of the

292
00:12:41,522 --> 00:12:42,322
number.

293
00:12:42,722 --> 00:12:45,282
It's about who the number describes.

294
00:12:49,082 --> 00:12:51,082
The first numbers you compute from data

295
00:12:51,162 --> 00:12:53,162
are usually parts of a whole.

296
00:12:53,853 --> 00:12:55,553
To get a part of a whole,

297
00:12:55,633 --> 00:12:57,393
you divide the part by the whole.

298
00:12:58,170 --> 00:13:00,410
Eighteen shaded boxes out of twenty four

299
00:13:00,570 --> 00:13:03,020
is zero point seven five,

300
00:13:03,060 --> 00:13:05,420
and statisticians call this a proportion.

301
00:13:06,151 --> 00:13:08,191
Can it ever be bigger than one?

302
00:13:08,917 --> 00:13:09,717
It can't.

303
00:13:10,273 --> 00:13:12,593
The answer always lands between zero and

304
00:13:12,673 --> 00:13:15,963
one, because the part sits inside the whole.

305
00:13:16,734 --> 00:13:19,649
So if you do get a number bigger than one,

306
00:13:19,729 --> 00:13:21,769
your fraction is upside down.

307
00:13:22,840 --> 00:13:25,971
A percent means out of a hundred.

308
00:13:26,051 --> 00:13:28,361
To turn a decimal into a percent,

309
00:13:28,441 --> 00:13:30,426
you multiply by a hundred,

310
00:13:30,506 --> 00:13:32,506
which moves the decimal point two places

311
00:13:32,586 --> 00:13:33,972
right.

312
00:13:34,012 --> 00:13:36,817
To go back, you divide by a hundred.

313
00:13:37,598 --> 00:13:40,088
Small percents are tricky.

314
00:13:40,168 --> 00:13:42,888
Eight percent is zero point zero eight,

315
00:13:43,088 --> 00:13:45,939
not zero point eight.

316
00:13:46,019 --> 00:13:48,019
The hardest part is usually picking the

317
00:13:48,219 --> 00:13:49,479
whole.

318
00:13:49,559 --> 00:13:51,159
You listen for the little word,

319
00:13:51,359 --> 00:13:52,159
of.

320
00:13:52,515 --> 00:13:54,895
Whatever group comes right after it...

321
00:13:54,975 --> 00:13:55,975
that's your whole.

322
00:13:56,636 --> 00:13:58,716
If the question asks what part of the pass

323
00:13:58,796 --> 00:14:01,786
holders, the whole is the pass holders.

324
00:14:02,462 --> 00:14:05,307
If it asks what part of all the students,

325
00:14:05,387 --> 00:14:06,747
then the whole is everyone.

326
00:14:08,194 --> 00:14:10,514
Lena digs into the forty pass holders,

327
00:14:10,634 --> 00:14:12,834
and thirty four of them said yes to evening

328
00:14:13,034 --> 00:14:13,834
hours.

329
00:14:14,114 --> 00:14:16,594
So what part of the pass holders want it?

330
00:14:17,270 --> 00:14:19,650
It's thirty four out of forty...

331
00:14:19,730 --> 00:14:21,610
which is eighty five percent.

332
00:14:22,336 --> 00:14:24,416
But what part of all the students surveyed

333
00:14:24,496 --> 00:14:26,336
are pass holders who said yes?

334
00:14:27,027 --> 00:14:29,092
It's the same thirty four...

335
00:14:29,172 --> 00:14:32,003
but now it goes over one hundred fifty,

336
00:14:32,083 --> 00:14:34,043
and that's about twenty three percent.

337
00:14:34,783 --> 00:14:37,529
It's the same part with a different whole,

338
00:14:37,569 --> 00:14:39,769
and it gives a very different answer.

339
00:14:41,105 --> 00:14:43,735
There's one more rule, which is to compare

340
00:14:43,975 --> 00:14:46,511
parts, not counts.

341
00:14:46,551 --> 00:14:50,276
Lena splits her yes answers by side of town.

342
00:14:50,356 --> 00:14:52,756
On the north side, forty two out of sixty

343
00:14:52,876 --> 00:14:54,471
said yes.

344
00:14:54,511 --> 00:14:57,106
On the south side, it was fifty four out

345
00:14:57,186 --> 00:14:58,226
of ninety.

346
00:14:58,306 --> 00:15:01,457
So does the south side want it more?

347
00:15:01,537 --> 00:15:05,717
It has more yeses, but it's a bigger group.

348
00:15:05,837 --> 00:15:09,662
So you divide each part by its own whole.

349
00:15:09,742 --> 00:15:12,132
The north comes to seventy percent,

350
00:15:12,172 --> 00:15:13,732
and the south comes to...

351
00:15:13,852 --> 00:15:15,732
sixty percent.

352
00:15:15,812 --> 00:15:18,292
A larger part of the north side wants evening

353
00:15:18,532 --> 00:15:19,923
hours.

354
00:15:20,003 --> 00:15:21,918
When groups differ in size,

355
00:15:21,998 --> 00:15:23,358
you compare percents.

356
00:15:24,485 --> 00:15:26,165
It's your turn one last time.

357
00:15:26,936 --> 00:15:28,776
In a class of eighty seniors,

358
00:15:28,856 --> 00:15:30,776
thirty two play a school sport,

359
00:15:30,856 --> 00:15:32,696
and twelve of those play two sports.

360
00:15:33,496 --> 00:15:35,656
What percent of the sport players play two

361
00:15:35,736 --> 00:15:37,176
sports?

362
00:15:37,336 --> 00:15:39,416
And what percent of all the seniors play

363
00:15:39,496 --> 00:15:40,296
two sports?

364
00:15:48,436 --> 00:15:50,992
For the sport players, the whole is the

365
00:15:51,032 --> 00:15:53,177
thirty two players.

366
00:15:53,257 --> 00:15:55,337
Twelve out of thirty two is thirty seven

367
00:15:55,497 --> 00:15:57,737
and a half percent.

368
00:15:57,817 --> 00:16:01,682
For all the seniors, the whole is eighty.

369
00:16:01,762 --> 00:16:04,162
Twelve out of eighty is fifteen percent.

370
00:16:04,948 --> 00:16:07,668
The trap is dividing by eighty for the first

371
00:16:07,748 --> 00:16:09,668
one, just because it's the biggest number

372
00:16:09,708 --> 00:16:10,708
on the page.

373
00:16:14,508 --> 00:16:16,028
This is the whole path.

374
00:16:16,813 --> 00:16:18,853
You start with a question whose answers

375
00:16:18,933 --> 00:16:22,094
vary, about a population.

376
00:16:22,134 --> 00:16:23,854
You take a random sample that looks like

377
00:16:23,934 --> 00:16:26,669
it, where rows are units and columns are

378
00:16:26,989 --> 00:16:30,825
variables, categorical or quantitative.

379
00:16:30,905 --> 00:16:32,640
You pick the right whole,

380
00:16:32,680 --> 00:16:34,720
and the sample's statistic estimates the

381
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,165
population's parameter.

382
00:16:37,205 --> 00:16:39,985
And you always say it in context...

383
00:16:40,145 --> 00:16:41,825
because the next sample would come out a

384
00:16:41,945 --> 00:16:42,865
bit different.

385
00:16:46,665 --> 00:16:49,590
A week later, Lena stands at the podium.

386
00:16:50,351 --> 00:16:53,152
She tells them: Sixty four percent of the

387
00:16:53,232 --> 00:16:55,072
one hundred fifty students I surveyed at

388
00:16:55,152 --> 00:16:57,392
random said they'd swim between six and

389
00:16:57,472 --> 00:16:58,272
nine.

390
00:16:58,827 --> 00:17:01,387
So nearly two in three of the school's students

391
00:17:01,547 --> 00:17:03,913
would, give or take a few points.

392
00:17:04,668 --> 00:17:06,268
So what does the council do?

393
00:17:06,964 --> 00:17:09,164
It votes for a two week trial.

394
00:17:09,880 --> 00:17:12,245
That Friday, the pool lights come on at

395
00:17:12,285 --> 00:17:14,761
dusk, and the water fills up.

396
00:17:15,432 --> 00:17:17,112
You'll find the notes and practice for this

397
00:17:17,312 --> 00:17:21,287
topic, free, on levlprep.com.

398
00:17:21,327 --> 00:17:23,998
And next time, we'll turn a yes or no column

399
00:17:24,078 --> 00:17:26,878
into a table and a graph that tell the story

400
00:17:26,918 --> 00:17:27,918
at a glance.

